# Checksum lists are machine-read, byte-exact files: git must write their line
# endings as LF and nothing else.
#
# With the default `core.autocrlf=true` on Windows, a plain checkout turns the
# LF blob into CRLF on disk. `sha256sum -c` then parses the recorded *file name*
# as "nyrqis-live.iso\r", cannot open it, and reports
#
#     sha256sum: 'nyrqis-live.iso'$'\r': No such file or directory
#
# so `tools/live_iso_selfcheck.sh` -- the negative control that proves the gate
# goes red on a broken disc -- fails on its own setup rather than on the defect
# it is meant to inject. The digest is also read straight out of these files by
# `tools/check_live_iso.sh`, so the bytes on disk have to be the bytes the
# build wrote.
#
# `text eol=lf` rather than `-text`, which would also stop the conversion: the
# two differ in what the repository sees. `-text` declares these files binary,
# so `git diff` on a changed digest prints "Binary files a/... and b/... differ"
# and a merge cannot show the conflicting lines -- and a one-line digest change
# is exactly what a reviewer needs to read. `eol=lf` keeps them text with LF
# line endings, and additionally normalises a CRLF blob if one is ever committed
# by a tool that writes CRLF itself, where `-text` would preserve the mistake
# and leave every future checkout wrong.
#
# This file gets the same rule: it is a rule list whose comments would otherwise
# pick up carriage returns on Windows, and whose diffs someone will want to read.
*.sha256 text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf
